# Weekly Chat Rule Review

## Goal
- Once per week, review the last 7 days of parent agent chats and identify recurring patterns that should become Cursor rules.

## Schedule
- Run this review every Monday before any other substantial coding task.
- If Monday is skipped, run at the next available session and mark it as a delayed weekly review.

## Weekly Process
1. Gather parent chat transcripts from the last 7 full calendar days.
2. Identify repeated mistakes, rework loops, or repeated user preferences.
3. Propose only narrow, single-purpose rules that reduce future rework.
4. Check overlap with existing rules and prefer refining an existing rule over creating duplicates.
5. Present each candidate rule with:
   - the repeated pattern it addresses
   - evidence references to at least 3 parent chats from that week
   - the expected behavior change
   - whether to create a new rule or update an existing one
6. Ask for explicit user approval before creating or modifying any `.cursor/rules/*.mdc` file.

## Candidate Rule Quality Bar
- Require clear evidence from at least 3 parent chats in the same week.
- For high-impact rules (safety, data loss, destructive commands), require either:
  - 2+ incidents with material risk, or
  - 1 incident with severe impact and a clear preventive rule.
- Avoid broad catch-all rules; keep each rule focused and testable in practice.
- Keep rule text concise, concrete, and action-oriented.
- Prefer safety and correctness rules before style-only guidance.

## Overlap And Duplication Check
- For each candidate rule, explicitly list overlapping existing rules (if any).
- Prefer updating an existing rule over creating a new one when overlap is substantial.
- New rules must include a one-line reason why an update was insufficient.

## Output Format For Weekly Review
1. Weekly findings summary:
   - pattern name
   - frequency count
   - linked parent chat references
2. Proposed rule changes:
   - new rule or update existing rule
   - target filename
   - one-sentence rationale
3. Draft `.mdc` text for each approved candidate.
4. Explicit approval request before any file edits.
5. Post-change note listing which rule files changed and why.
